1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,490
Type one question into ChatGPT tonight.

2
00:00:03,030 --> 00:00:04,940
Before the first word comes back,

3
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:07,280
your question lands in a building full of chips,

4
00:00:07,450 --> 00:00:09,360
all doing math at the same time.

5
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:10,990
Most of the world's AI

6
00:00:10,990 --> 00:00:12,740
chips come from one company.

7
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:15,360
A company that started in a Denny's,

8
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:16,890
making chips for video games.

9
00:00:17,610 --> 00:00:19,150
Nvidia isn't an AI company

10
00:00:19,150 --> 00:00:20,720
that used to make gaming chips.

11
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:23,350
It's a gaming company whose math turned

12
00:00:23,350 --> 00:00:24,940
out to be the math of AI.

13
00:00:25,860 --> 00:00:27,830
And the size of it breaks your brain.

14
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:30,890
When Nvidia went public in 1999,

15
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:33,690
it raised about 42 million dollars.

16
00:00:34,430 --> 00:00:34,900
Today,

17
00:00:34,910 --> 00:00:36,210
it takes in that much money

18
00:00:36,210 --> 00:00:37,540
in less than an hour.

19
00:00:38,390 --> 00:00:40,350
It's worth about 5 trillion dollars,

20
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:42,240
the most valuable company on Earth.

21
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:44,260
And the man who runs it once cleaned

22
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:46,330
toilets at a school for troubled kids.

23
00:00:47,470 --> 00:00:49,000
Here's what should be impossible.

24
00:00:49,780 --> 00:00:50,720
In the nineties,

25
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:51,800
dozens of companies were

26
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:52,970
making graphics chips.

27
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:55,420
Nvidia's first chip flopped.

28
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:57,290
It came within weeks of running out of money.

29
00:00:57,840 --> 00:00:58,760
The king of the industry

30
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:01,250
was a company called 3dfx.

31
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:03,990
Nvidia ended up buying what was left of it.

32
00:01:04,810 --> 00:01:06,150
So how did a chip for

33
00:01:06,150 --> 00:01:08,000
video games end up running AI?

34
00:01:08,790 --> 00:01:09,770
Why did Wall Street hate

35
00:01:09,770 --> 00:01:11,100
the bet that made it rich?

36
00:01:11,990 --> 00:01:14,180
And can anything knock it off the top?

37
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:17,290
This is the story of a booth at Denny's,

38
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:18,420
a name a toilet paper

39
00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:19,710
company got to first,

40
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:21,250
a rescue from Sega,

41
00:01:21,890 --> 00:01:23,030
and two gaming cards

42
00:01:23,030 --> 00:01:25,780
at a student's house that changed computers forever.

43
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:28,430
The whole history of Nvidia.

44
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:34,020
It starts in 1973,

45
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:36,100
when a nine-year-old named Jensen

46
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:37,710
Huang is sent to America.

47
00:01:38,370 --> 00:01:40,530
His relatives enroll him at what they think

48
00:01:40,530 --> 00:01:42,560
is a fancy boarding school in Kentucky.

49
00:01:43,230 --> 00:01:45,140
It's actually a school for troubled kids.

50
00:01:45,370 --> 00:01:46,900
His job is cleaning the toilets.

51
00:01:47,290 --> 00:01:47,900
Every day.

52
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:49,650
His roommate is seventeen,

53
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:50,970
covered in tattoos,

54
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:52,050
and can't read.

55
00:01:52,610 --> 00:01:54,280
So Jensen teaches him to read,

56
00:01:54,490 --> 00:01:56,600
and the roommate teaches Jensen to bench press.

57
00:01:57,170 --> 00:01:57,820
At fifteen,

58
00:01:57,930 --> 00:01:59,700
he gets a job at a Denny's in Oregon.

59
00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:00,940
Washing dishes,

60
00:02:01,190 --> 00:02:02,120
then bussing tables,

61
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:03,020
then waiting them.

62
00:02:03,450 --> 00:02:04,590
Remember Denny's.

63
00:02:04,590 --> 00:02:05,460
It comes back.

64
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:07,730
He studies electrical engineering,

65
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:09,480
and by his late twenties he's

66
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:11,430
designing chips in Silicon Valley.

67
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:13,030
In late 1992,

68
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:14,650
he sits down with two engineers,

69
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:16,680
Chris Malachowsky and Curtis Priem,

70
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:17,910
at a Denny's in San Jose.

71
00:02:18,510 --> 00:02:19,100
Coffee,

72
00:02:19,150 --> 00:02:19,540
eggs,

73
00:02:19,790 --> 00:02:20,760
hours of talking.

74
00:02:21,270 --> 00:02:21,740
The plan:

75
00:02:21,870 --> 00:02:23,780
a chip that makes video games look real.

76
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:25,430
In April 1993,

77
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:28,663
they start the company with about 40 thousand dollars.

78
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:30,030
They don't have a name yet,

79
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:31,520
so they label their files NV,

80
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:32,850
for next version.

81
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:35,580
They want to call it NVision.

82
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:38,250
But a toilet paper company already has it.

83
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:39,760
So Jensen picks a word

84
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:41,090
with an N and a V:

85
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:41,970
invidia,

86
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:43,355
Latin for envy.

87
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:45,270
Then he pitches his old boss,

88
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:47,850
who calls it one of the worst pitches he's ever heard,

89
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:50,030
and phones a famous investor anyway.

90
00:02:50,770 --> 00:02:51,700
That investor,

91
00:02:51,830 --> 00:02:53,550
Don Valentine of Sequoia,

92
00:02:53,550 --> 00:02:54,230
puts money in,

93
00:02:54,230 --> 00:02:55,220
with a warning.

94
00:02:55,700 --> 00:02:56,860
If you lose my money,

95
00:02:56,860 --> 00:02:57,830
I will kill you.

96
00:03:02,270 --> 00:03:03,490
In 1995,

97
00:03:03,490 --> 00:03:05,240
Nvidia's first chip comes out.

98
00:03:05,550 --> 00:03:06,880
It's called NV1,

99
00:03:06,910 --> 00:03:08,960
and it builds 3D worlds out of curves.

100
00:03:09,630 --> 00:03:10,860
Everyone else picks triangles.

101
00:03:11,310 --> 00:03:12,230
And when Microsoft builds

102
00:03:12,230 --> 00:03:14,360
its game graphics around triangles too,

103
00:03:14,510 --> 00:03:15,740
the curve bet is dead.

104
00:03:16,220 --> 00:03:17,400
Nvidia is also building

105
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:19,230
a chip for Sega's next console,

106
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:20,310
the same wrong way.

107
00:03:20,810 --> 00:03:22,540
So Jensen does something wild.

108
00:03:22,650 --> 00:03:24,110
He tells Sega to find another partner,

109
00:03:24,110 --> 00:03:26,380
and asks them to pay Nvidia anyway.

110
00:03:26,850 --> 00:03:27,950
Sega's American boss,

111
00:03:27,950 --> 00:03:29,760
Shoichiro Irimajiri,

112
00:03:29,830 --> 00:03:30,620
says yes.

113
00:03:30,710 --> 00:03:32,070
5 million dollars.

114
00:03:32,070 --> 00:03:34,320
Enough to stay alive for about six months.

115
00:03:34,830 --> 00:03:36,650
Nvidia lays off about half its people

116
00:03:36,650 --> 00:03:39,480
and bets everything on one chip that draws triangles,

117
00:03:39,730 --> 00:03:40,600
the RIVA 128.

118
00:03:41,310 --> 00:03:43,420
It ships in August 1997,

119
00:03:43,630 --> 00:03:45,880
with about one month of payroll left in the bank.

120
00:03:46,330 --> 00:03:47,350
It sells about a million

121
00:03:47,350 --> 00:03:48,880
units in four months.

122
00:03:49,430 --> 00:03:50,690
And one sentence becomes

123
00:03:50,690 --> 00:03:52,440
the company's unofficial motto.

124
00:03:53,050 --> 00:03:54,490
Our company is thirty days

125
00:03:54,490 --> 00:03:55,780
from going out of business.

126
00:04:00,190 --> 00:04:01,390
On January 22nd,

127
00:04:01,390 --> 00:04:02,520
1999,

128
00:04:02,610 --> 00:04:04,860
Nvidia goes public at 12 dollars a share.

129
00:04:05,330 --> 00:04:06,360
Remember that 12 dollars.

130
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:09,300
That same year it launches the GeForce 256,

131
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:11,410
and sells it with a brand new word:

132
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:12,730
the world's first GPU.

133
00:04:13,550 --> 00:04:15,030
So what is a GPU,

134
00:04:15,030 --> 00:04:15,740
actually?

135
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:18,970
To draw one frame of a game on an HD screen,

136
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:20,830
you color about two million pixels.

137
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:22,510
Then you do it again,

138
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:23,850
sixty times a second.

139
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:25,410
A normal processor,

140
00:04:25,620 --> 00:04:26,270
a CPU,

141
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:28,290
is like a few brilliant professors.

142
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:29,500
They can solve anything,

143
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:31,530
but one problem at a time.

144
00:04:32,010 --> 00:04:33,850
A GPU is a stadium full

145
00:04:33,850 --> 00:04:36,180
of kids who can only do simple sums.

146
00:04:36,430 --> 00:04:38,320
But they all do them at the same moment.

147
00:04:39,220 --> 00:04:41,150
One kid adds a little red to this pixel.

148
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:42,710
Another darkens that one.

149
00:04:42,740 --> 00:04:43,750
Two million kids,

150
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:44,790
one sum each,

151
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:45,310
done.

152
00:04:45,810 --> 00:04:46,840
For coloring pixels,

153
00:04:47,070 --> 00:04:47,980
the stadium wins.

154
00:04:48,250 --> 00:04:49,100
Every time.

155
00:04:49,790 --> 00:04:50,580
In 2000,

156
00:04:50,690 --> 00:04:53,300
Nvidia agrees to buy the remains of 3dfx,

157
00:04:53,510 --> 00:04:55,600
the company that used to rule gaming graphics.

158
00:04:56,250 --> 00:04:57,300
Earlier that year,

159
00:04:57,370 --> 00:04:58,790
Microsoft picked Nvidia for

160
00:04:58,790 --> 00:05:00,380
the graphics in its first Xbox,

161
00:05:00,670 --> 00:05:02,900
and paid 200 million dollars up front.

162
00:05:03,660 --> 00:05:05,520
And when the stock hits 100 dollars,

163
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:06,800
Jensen gets the company logo

164
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:08,390
tattooed on his body.

165
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:13,190
Meanwhile,

166
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:13,870
at Stanford,

167
00:05:14,020 --> 00:05:15,280
a grad student named Ian Buck

168
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:17,050
is wiring GeForce cards together.

169
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:19,180
He builds a language that lets them do

170
00:05:19,180 --> 00:05:21,090
math that has nothing to do with games.

171
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:23,710
Nvidia hires him in 2004.

172
00:05:24,250 --> 00:05:25,090
In 2006,

173
00:05:25,090 --> 00:05:26,920
Nvidia announces CUDA.

174
00:05:27,270 --> 00:05:29,550
It turns that stadium of kids loose on any problem:

175
00:05:29,550 --> 00:05:30,090
weather,

176
00:05:30,090 --> 00:05:30,840
medicine,

177
00:05:30,930 --> 00:05:31,280
physics.

178
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:32,960
And it builds CUDA into

179
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:34,530
its ordinary gaming chips.

180
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:36,980
Millions of gamers carry a supercomputer

181
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:38,670
feature they will never use.

182
00:05:39,170 --> 00:05:39,880
The idea:

183
00:05:40,050 --> 00:05:42,560
put the tool in millions of computers first,

184
00:05:42,950 --> 00:05:45,180
and the people who need it will find it.

185
00:05:45,610 --> 00:05:46,960
Wall Street sees the bill,

186
00:05:47,090 --> 00:05:47,920
not the point.

187
00:05:48,570 --> 00:05:49,530
In 2008,

188
00:05:49,530 --> 00:05:50,790
Nvidia takes a charge of about

189
00:05:50,790 --> 00:05:53,330
200 million dollars for faulty laptop chips,

190
00:05:53,330 --> 00:05:54,470
and the stock falls

191
00:05:54,470 --> 00:05:56,220
31 percent in one day.

192
00:05:56,670 --> 00:05:57,970
By the end of that year,

193
00:05:57,970 --> 00:05:59,050
it has lost about three

194
00:05:59,050 --> 00:06:00,560
quarters of its value.

195
00:06:01,530 --> 00:06:03,020
Remember thirty days from dead?

196
00:06:03,190 --> 00:06:03,710
This time,

197
00:06:03,710 --> 00:06:05,720
Nvidia keeps paying for CUDA anyway.

198
00:06:06,220 --> 00:06:07,180
A tool for a market

199
00:06:07,180 --> 00:06:08,750
that doesn't exist yet.

200
00:06:13,070 --> 00:06:13,550
Then,

201
00:06:13,550 --> 00:06:14,440
in 2012,

202
00:06:14,670 --> 00:06:16,070
the market finally shows up,

203
00:06:16,070 --> 00:06:17,700
at a student's parents' house.

204
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:19,350
Alex Krizhevsky,

205
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:21,520
his friend Ilya Sutskever and their

206
00:06:21,520 --> 00:06:23,540
professor Geoffrey Hinton want to

207
00:06:23,540 --> 00:06:25,390
teach a computer to recognize pictures.

208
00:06:26,040 --> 00:06:27,060
They train a neural network

209
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:28,990
on two Nvidia gaming cards,

210
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:30,160
GTX 580s,

211
00:06:30,160 --> 00:06:31,090
for about a week.

212
00:06:31,530 --> 00:06:33,130
It enters the biggest image contest

213
00:06:33,130 --> 00:06:35,300
in the world and wins by a mile:

214
00:06:35,350 --> 00:06:36,920
about 15 percent wrong,

215
00:06:36,990 --> 00:06:39,180
when the next best was 26.

216
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:40,660
The math inside a neural network

217
00:06:40,660 --> 00:06:43,570
is the same kind of simple sums as coloring pixels.

218
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:44,740
Millions of them,

219
00:06:44,740 --> 00:06:45,730
all at once.

220
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:48,330
The stadium of kids was built for games.

221
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:49,950
It was perfect for AI.

222
00:06:50,650 --> 00:06:52,800
You may have heard Nvidia just got lucky with AI.

223
00:06:53,390 --> 00:06:54,000
Partly true.

224
00:06:54,250 --> 00:06:55,430
But luck only pays if

225
00:06:55,430 --> 00:06:56,860
you're already standing there.

226
00:06:57,520 --> 00:06:59,090
Jensen goes all in.

227
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:00,190
In 2016,

228
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:02,253
he hand-delivers the first DGX-1,

229
00:07:02,253 --> 00:07:03,380
an AI computer,

230
00:07:03,380 --> 00:07:04,680
to a small lab

231
00:07:04,680 --> 00:07:06,670
in San Francisco called OpenAI.

232
00:07:07,340 --> 00:07:08,140
He signs it:

233
00:07:08,140 --> 00:07:10,710
to the future of computing and humanity.

234
00:07:11,340 --> 00:07:13,740
One of OpenAI's founders is Ilya Sutskever.

235
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:15,170
From the student's house.

236
00:07:15,670 --> 00:07:17,720
Then crypto miners buy up gaming cards,

237
00:07:18,090 --> 00:07:19,360
crypto crashes in 2018,

238
00:07:19,850 --> 00:07:21,250
and Nvidia's stock loses more than

239
00:07:21,250 --> 00:07:22,580
half its value in months.

240
00:07:23,170 --> 00:07:24,990
Regulators later fine it 5.5 million

241
00:07:24,990 --> 00:07:26,750
dollars for not telling investors how

242
00:07:26,750 --> 00:07:28,360
much crypto was behind its sales.

243
00:07:32,710 --> 00:07:33,790
On November 30th,

244
00:07:33,790 --> 00:07:34,880
2022,

245
00:07:34,890 --> 00:07:37,120
OpenAI releases ChatGPT.

246
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:39,920
It was trained on a Microsoft supercomputer built

247
00:07:39,920 --> 00:07:42,050
from tens of thousands of Nvidia chips.

248
00:07:42,630 --> 00:07:43,870
Suddenly every tech giant

249
00:07:43,870 --> 00:07:45,640
on Earth wants the same thing,

250
00:07:45,890 --> 00:07:47,700
and one company sells most of them.

251
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:48,760
In a gold rush,

252
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:50,150
sell shovels.

253
00:07:50,730 --> 00:07:52,290
Nvidia's top AI chip,

254
00:07:52,290 --> 00:07:53,060
the H100,

255
00:07:53,470 --> 00:07:55,650
sells for tens of thousands of dollars each,

256
00:07:55,650 --> 00:07:57,820
and buyers wait months to get one.

257
00:07:58,230 --> 00:07:59,580
In May 2023,

258
00:07:59,590 --> 00:08:01,500
Nvidia is worth a trillion dollars.

259
00:08:01,890 --> 00:08:03,260
Denny's puts a plaque on the booth:

260
00:08:03,330 --> 00:08:05,740
the booth that launched a 1 trillion dollar company.

261
00:08:06,180 --> 00:08:07,390
In June 2024,

262
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:09,320
it passes Microsoft and Apple to become

263
00:08:09,320 --> 00:08:11,350
the most valuable company in the world.

264
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:12,200
Then,

265
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:13,370
in January 2025,

266
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:15,660
a Chinese lab called DeepSeek shows

267
00:08:15,660 --> 00:08:17,110
off a cheap AI model,

268
00:08:17,140 --> 00:08:19,580
and Nvidia loses about 589 billion

269
00:08:19,580 --> 00:08:21,250
dollars of value in one day.

270
00:08:21,690 --> 00:08:22,810
The biggest one-day loss

271
00:08:22,810 --> 00:08:24,260
in stock market history.

272
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:26,170
And then it just keeps climbing.

273
00:08:26,580 --> 00:08:28,230
4 trillion in July 2025.

274
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:29,890
5 trillion that October.

275
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:32,190
The first company ever to reach either one.

276
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:38,010
Which brings us to right now.

277
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:39,570
October 2026.

278
00:08:40,160 --> 00:08:41,250
In its last quarter,

279
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:45,120
Nvidia brought in 96.2 billion dollars.

280
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:47,550
Data centers alone were 89 billion.

281
00:08:48,350 --> 00:08:50,550
That's more than nine of every ten dollars.

282
00:08:50,550 --> 00:08:52,880
The gaming company now mostly sells to AI.

283
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:54,490
Its newest chips,

284
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:55,410
Vera Rubin,

285
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:56,320
are in full production,

286
00:08:56,320 --> 00:08:59,040
and it expects 108 billion this quarter,

287
00:08:59,040 --> 00:09:01,730
without counting any AI chip sales to China.

